{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"After an override is reset and the daemon restarts, a resumed run can use the removed model instead of the restored routing policy."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A prior routing edit can cause later rounds to discard an escalated model and return to a weaker one, potentially wasting work or missing a successful result."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A persisted override referencing a removed model or provider can prevent daemon startup, while the CLI reset requires the daemon to be running."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"nuisance","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An unrelated routing edit can reorder queued invocations and create cancellation records, but the affected calls are retried."},{"id":"C5","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Invalid routing input produces a hard-to-read validation dump that still contains the cell and reason."},{"id":"C7","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"The eval diff can falsely report an effective routing change and lead an operator to make a decision based on an outcome that will not occur."},{"id":"C8","consequence":"nuisance","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Resetting an absent override adds empty history entries, broadcasts updates, and can cause queued work to be retried."}]}